296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 267 0 R
/E (Electrooculography provides a practical way to use eye activity as an input signal for computer interaction. This paper describes a low-cost EOG-based prototype developed to recognize four ocular states: neutral gaze, upward eye movement, downward eye movement, and blinking. The hardware consists of a BioAmp EXG Pill connected to an ESP32 microcontroller. Vertical EOG is recorded using electrodes positioned above and below the right eye, while the ESP32 samples the signal at 500 Hz, performs initial filtering, and transmits the processed values to a computer.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 267 0 R
/E (The available dataset contains 64,000 samples collected across 160 trials, with 40 trials for each class. Every trial lasts 0.8 s and contains 400 samples. A set of 28 handcrafted features was extracted from each trial, covering statistical properties, signal trend, peak behavior, spectral content, and Hjorth mobility. Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and radial-basis-function Support Vector Machine classifiers were evaluated.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 267 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 267 0 R
/E (The classifier was also integrated into a real-time control program. Upward and downward predictions move the cursor vertically, while a double blink is mapped to a mouse click. Prediction confidence, short- term voting, and an action cooldown are used to reduce unstable commands. The results show that a relatively inexpensive EOG setup can support a functioning machine-learning-based interaction prototype. At the same time, the current dataset does not contain participant identifiers, so subject-independent performance cannot be assessed.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
