306 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 268 0 R
/E (The electrical potential between the front and back of the eye changes relative to electrodes placed around the eye as gaze direction changes. This makes EOG suitable for detecting intentional eye movements and blinking [Chang, 2019]. Previous studies have used EOG for cursor control, virtual keyboards, mobility systems, communication aids, and other hands-free interfaces [Chang, 2019; Belkhiria et al., 2022].)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
307 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 268 0 R
/E (EOG also comes with practical difficulties. The recorded signal can drift over time, change with electrode placement, pick up muscle and electrical interference, and vary between sessions and users. Blinks may be intentional or involuntary, and small directional movements can be difficult to separate reliably. For these reasons, a useful EOG system requires more than simple signal acquisition. The processing and evaluation strategy matters as much as the hardware.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
308 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 268 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 268 0 R
/E (The present work explores a smaller command vocabulary using inexpensive hardware. The system recognizes Neutral, Up, Down, and Blink states from vertical EOG recordings and maps those states to a basic computer-control interface.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
