319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 269 0 R
/E (The work does not introduce a new classifier or a new EOG sensing principle. Its value lies in the complete low-cost pipeline and in the evaluation of how well that pipeline performs on the collected data.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 269 0 R
/E (EOG measures changes in the electrical potential around the eye as the eye moves. A common engineering approximation treats the eye as an electrical dipole, with the corneal side relatively positive compared with the retinal side [Chang, 2019]. When the direction of gaze changes, the voltage measured by nearby electrodes also changes.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
321 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 269 0 R
/E (Electrode position determines which movements can be measured most directly. Electrodes placed to the left and right of the eyes are commonly used for horizontal movement. Electrodes placed above and below an eye are suitable for vertical movement and also capture strong responses during blinking.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
